    "video_id": "kenji:gGNJbFQIr5g",
    "purpose": "Review and rank about a dozen outdoor standalone pizza ovens after two months of near-daily testing, giving picks for budget, high-volume, wood-fired and overall best.",
    "video_type": "ranking",
    "thesis": "Outdoor ovens can reach the 900°F needed for Neapolitan-style pizza that home ovens can't. The video counts down four picks, from the budget Pizzeria Pronto to the top choice, the Roccbox.",
    "key_takeaways": [
      "The Pizzeria Pronto (about $200) is the budget pick. It is propane-fired with a double pizza stone, reaches roughly 600–700°F air and 550°F floor, and bakes crispy pizzas in about 5 minutes, but it won't give Neapolitan leopard spotting.",
      "The Blackstone (about $285) has a rotating stone and passes 900°F, cooking pizzas in 70–90 seconds. Build quality was poor: the motor failed, the stone cracked and the paint flaked, though fixes like a standard rotisserie motor and a thrust bearing are discussed.",
      "The Uuni 3 ($300) burns wood pellets and reaches 900°F+ dome and about 750°F floor. It has a learning curve with fire management, but it is the most portable at about 25 lb, and a gas conversion kit was rumored.",
      "The Roccbox (about $600 shipped) is the top pick. It is solidly built, runs on wood/charcoal or a swappable gas burner, cooks Neapolitan pizza in 75–90 seconds, stays cool to the touch, and comes with a perforated aluminum peel. Its downsides are a 12-inch mouth limiting pizzas to about 11 inches, and the price."
    ],
